                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1386-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_wc.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Wide Cardioid" title="Wide Cardioid" ></span>Oktava MK-101</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Wide Cardioid Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The MK-101 is a large diaphragm, side-address condenser built upon the successful MK-012 transformerless circuitry. The MK-101 is actually a detachable capsule head; it can be screwed on to any MC-012/MK-012 head amplifier.</p><p>The 101 head contains the large-diaphragm capsule from the MK-219/MK-319. This is a 34.5mm, center-terminated capsule (inaccurately described in some cases as a &#8220;26mm capsule&#8221;).</p><blockquote><p><cite>Michael Joly, 2008</cite><br />Like the MK-219 and MK-319 (which use the same capsule), the MK-101 makes a great male or female vocal mic where the recordist needs minimum sibilance but a clear midrange presence. In fact, the &#8216;101 head driving the transformerless &#8216;012 body actually reveals some undiscovered &#8216;air&#8217; this capsule is capable of hearing. I&#8217;ve received a number of reports of clients using this mic for drum OH too &#8212; large diaphragm &#8220;size&#8221; but with the transient response and clarity of the transformerless circuit.</p></blockquote><p><p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Oktava/MK-101">Oktava MK-101</a> is also known as: MK101.</p><p>The mic was released in 2004.</p>
